{
  "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
  "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 1:01 p.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Under the deferential review required by the ADPA, Mr. Mirzayance was not entitled to Federal habeas corpus relief on his ineffective counsel claim because the State court adjudication of that claim was not contrary to, nor an unreasonable application of, the clearly established Strickland test."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "At some point during the oral argument, and perhaps at the beginning because it is the beginning inquiry: When there is an evidentiary hearing, how does the standard for the court of appeals differ than when there has been no evidentiary hearing?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0000-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The point here under the ADPA -- and I think it is Congress's clear intent -- is that all of these claims are to be funneled through the State courts first. And Congress has entrusted the State courts to be the primary and first interpreters and enforcers of Federal constitutional law for State prisoners' claims."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0000-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And every bit of Mirzayance's own deeds and words show that he did. Before the killing itself, he closed the curtains and waited until he was alone with the victim before entering her room with a gun in his pocket and the silent weapon drawn. He struck with the silent weapon, delivering fatal blows, resorting to the gun only when she screamed and struggled, and then immediately collected the shell casings, turned off the lights, collected the knife, went back to his apartment where he showered, disposed of the bloody clothes, concocted a false alibi message on the machine -- excuse me -- and then, overcome with guilt at the wrongfulness of his conduct, he calls his friend and says: \"I messed up big-time.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But as far as I can tell, the \"nothing to lose\" issue has not been addressed by us and is not clearly established. So why do we look at it at the latter level of generality as opposed to the former?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Excuse me. The issue is not whether it's unreasonable or not. The issue is whether it's an unreasonable application of -- of clearly established Supreme Court law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Mercer",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, what I say is that this Court has held that Strickland generally applies to almost all ineffective counsel cases. And certainly this Court has stated it applies in specific type issues of conduct."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0001-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So they say: We did conduct that record independent review. And our conclusion is that it was unreasonable, okay? Nothing to do with any special rule here or anything. We just think it was unreasonable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is that your road map?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Sevilla",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I would like to begin by addressing the so-called \"nothing to lose\" rule, which is a fiction attributed to Profitt v. Waldron, which -- it does not say that in Profitt v. Waldron. It's a fiction attributed to the Ninth Circuit, because the Ninth Circuit not only did not say that; they rejected the idea that they were relying on a \"nothing to lose\" rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Where in the record does it\nshow that he misunderstood the law, as opposed to making\na practical evaluation of what the jury was likely to\ndo?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: He didn't use the word\n\"functional equivalent\" in the portions I read. Maybe\nyou can correct me if I'm wrong about that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Sevilla",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SEVILLA: He's got nothing to lose and\nsomething to gain. There was no benefit in taking the\n-- the action that he took in waiving this defense,\nwhich was credible."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Mercer",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First off, there was no finding that\nAttorney Wager misunderstood California law or somehow\nwas under the impression that a finding of first degree\nmurder was legally precluded a finding of NGI. He\nargued at the State trial that there's no question that\nan insane person can deliberate, and surely while the\njury's verdict was as devastating under the facts of\nthis case, he didn't close up his books and go home."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Mercer",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0002-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the aggregate sentence is 29 years\nto life. It's 25 years to life for the first degree\nmurder, plus four years for the weapons enhancement in\nthis term, a mid-term, because Wager successfully argued\nthat his client was remorseful."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-1315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "70d39f661b3443e17daab11b2e8dfa74e4d148a891aa40d7df2a6159a34f5393",
      "evidence_id": "ev-70d39f661b34-0002-0005",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted.\n(Whereupon, at 2:02 p.m., the case in the\nabove-entitled matter was submitted.)"
    },
    {
